expl=['The first law conserves charge and the second conserves energy; swapping them (C) reverses their roles.','Charge is conserved at junctions and energy around loops; B wrongly assigns energy to the first law.','Parallel branches have equal p.d. by the loop law. B is true but follows from the junction law, while D adds parallel p.d.s.','The signed net e.m.f. is $6.3+9.6-8.4=7.5$ V; each resistor drops $7.5/3=2.5$ V, so $V_{PQ}=9.6-2.5=7.1$ V. B misses the 9.6 V rise.','The opposing 4.0 V cell makes net e.m.f. $3.0+2.0-4.0=1.0$ V; $I=1.0/5.0=0.20$ A. D adds all cell e.m.f.s.','Five cubes in series give $5(4.0)=20$ ohm per row and two such rows in parallel give 10 ohm. D treats all ten as series.','Equal junction potentials give $R_T/3.00=4.80/2.00$, hence $R_T=7.20$ ohm; two equal 14.4 ohm branches yield this. C confuses $R_T$ with the unknown branch.','Closing the switch puts L2 and L3 in parallel, so L1 takes more voltage and L2 less; C reverses the changes.','Branch currents are inversely proportional to 1, 2 and 3 identical resistors: $A_3=1.2$ A, $A_4=0.4$ A and total $A_2=2.2$ A. C overstates the two-resistor branch current.','The loop law gives $V_2=V_3+V_4$, so D is zero. B incorrectly sums both paths.','One reversed cell leaves net e.m.f. $E$ across three equal series resistors, so each gets $E/3$. D ignores the sharing.','The two series pairs are parallel: $I_1(R_1+R_2)=I_2(R_3+R_4)$, giving B. C adds the drops of separate parallel paths.']
